\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich expression belongs in the blank when the upper and lower surfaces are parametrized as graphs over $D$ and their outward flux integrands are added pointwise where defined, before integration?" 13194139533508 17592186405019 true] [17592186198625 1071 "![](images/e6/e61f3d7c23ef7876bec2f11c93974551058bcd32f28745898604926d84dbcfee.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨y,\\,0,\\,2x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533508 17592186405019 false] [17592186198625 1071 "![](images/e6/e61f3d7c23ef7876bec2f11c93974551058bcd32f28745898604926d84dbcfee.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨y,\\,0,\\,2x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186198625 1072 #uuid "74d7a79e-d57c-5768-84fc-b3ae4dae67d9" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198625 1074 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨y,0,2x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower cone ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2)=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=2x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-0\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186198626 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194357 13194139533507 17592186045585 false] [17592186198626 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194357 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198626 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406452 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186198626 1069 "q-161863" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198626 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186198626 1071 "Consider the vector field $F = ⟨1, z, 0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the parabolic cylinders $z = 4x^{2}$ and $z = 16 - 4y^{2}$. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(D)A A A A A A AdA$.\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533507 17592186045585 false] [17592186198626 1071 "Consider the vector field $F = ⟨1, z, 0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the parabolic cylinders $z = 4x^{2}$ and $z = 16 - 4y^{2}$. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(D)A A A A A A AdA$.\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198626 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,z,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the parabolic cylinders $z=4{x}^{2}$ and $z=16-4{y}^{2}.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich expression belongs in the blank when the upper and lower surfaces are parametrized as graphs over $D$ and their outward flux integrands are added pointwise where defined, before integration?" 13194139533508 17592186405019 true] [17592186198626 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,z,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the parabolic cylinders $z=4{x}^{2}$ and $z=16-4{y}^{2}.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533508 17592186405019 false] [17592186198626 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,z,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the parabolic cylinders $z=4{x}^{2}$ and $z=16-4{y}^{2}.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186198626 1072 #uuid "87ce70cc-a7a3-5bf0-ac23-45c7dbb8a56d" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198626 1074 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=16-4{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=4{x}^{2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cylinder ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(16-4{y}^{2})=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(16-4{y}^{2})=-8y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=z=16-4{y}^{2},\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 0-(16-4{y}^{2})\\cdot (-8y)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(128y-32{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower cylinder ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2})=8x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=z=4{x}^{2},\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 8x-4{x}^{2}\\cdot 0+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}-8x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(128y-32{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}8x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}128y-32{y}^{3}+8x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406399 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406398 1069 "q-115261" 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406398 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406398 1071 "Given that the tangent line approximation to a differentiable function $f(x)$ at every point in an open interval containing $x=\\sqrt{2}$ strictly overestimates the values of the function at every other point in that interval, which of the following must be true?" 13194139533508 17592186405019 true] [17592186406398 1071 "Given that the tangent line approximation to the function $f(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{2}$ overestimates the values of the function in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2},$ which of the following must be true?" 13194139533508 17592186405019 false] [17592186406398 1071 "Given that the tangent line approximation to the function $f(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{2}$ overestimates the values of the function in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2},$ which of the following must be true?" 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406398 1072 #uuid "1aea0f50-2894-540a-8561-3e930ff1c7d7" 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406398 1074 "Since the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{2}$ overestimates the values of the function, we have\n\n$$\nL(x)>f(x),\n$$\n\nthat is, the line $L(x)$ must be above the graph of $f(x)$ near the point  $x=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the function must be concave down in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533508 17592186405019 false] [17592186406398 1074 "Since the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{2}$ overestimates the values of the function, we have\n\n$$\nL(x)>f(x),\n$$\n\nthat is, the line $L(x)$ must be above the graph of $f(x)$ near the point  $x=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the function must be concave down in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406398 1074 "Since the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at every point $a$ in the given neighborhood strictly overestimates the values of the function at every other point, we have\n\n$$\nL_a(x)>f(x)\\quad (x\\ne a),\n$$\n\nthat is, each tangent line $L_a(x)$ lies above the graph of $f(x)$ throughout the neighborhood, with equality only at $x=a.$ A differentiable function whose graph lies below each of its tangent lines on an interval is concave down on that interval.\n\nTherefore, the function must be concave down in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533508 17592186405019 true] [17592186406399 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406400 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406399 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406401 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406399 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406402 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406399 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406403 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406399 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406404 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406399 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406399 1077 "selection" 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406399 1079 #edb/ref 17592186406400 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406399 1080 #uuid "76a1b764-1da7-5a71-9954-47bcc426429c" 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406400 1081 "$f(x)$ is concave down in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2}$" 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406400 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406400 1084 #uuid "827d4a3d-b6ab-53b6-bb12-f25faa0e6566" 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406401 1081 "$f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=\\sqrt{2}$" 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406401 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406401 1084 #uuid "383467c9-40c4-5db5-8d12-a8601454a694" 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406402 1081 "$f(x)$ has a global minimum at $x=\\sqrt{2}$" 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406402 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406402 1084 #uuid "7e4d8f11-3750-50df-8e99-85f1b881ad96" 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406403 1081 "$f(x)$ is concave up in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2}$" 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406403 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406403 1084 #uuid "9fcfcbd6-75df-589e-b6e8-48082215ad4e" 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406404 1081 "$f(x)$ is a linear function" 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406404 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406404 1084 #uuid "ffd3cc61-fa12-54a5-8dfc-015b9b476459" 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406406 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406405 1069 "q-123316" 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406405 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406405 1071 "Which of the following sequences diverge?\n\nI. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{1-{n}^{2}}$ for $n\\ge 1$\nII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3}{n}$ for $n\\ge 1$\nIII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{2{n}^{2}-3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}+5}$ for $n\\ge 1$" 13194139533508 17592186405019 false] [17592186406405 1071 "Which of the following sequences diverge?\n\nI. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{1-{n}^{2}}$ for $n\\ge 1$\nII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3}{n}$ for $n\\ge 1$\nIII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{2{n}^{2}-3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}+5}$ for $n\\ge 1$" 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406405 1071 "Which of the following sequences diverge?\n\nI. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{1-{n}^{2}}$ for $n\\ge 2$\nII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3}{n}$ for $n\\ge 1$\nIII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{2{n}^{2}-3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}+5}$ for $n\\ge 1$" 13194139533508 17592186405019 true] [17592186406405 1072 #uuid "fd4fed36-e750-5401-88f4-2ac9e7e598e7" 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406405 1074 "Let's analyze each sequence in turn. We need to calculate the limit of each sequence as $n$ approaches infinity.\n\n- In sequence I,  the dominant term in the denominator is $-{n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{1-{n}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{0-1} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges.\n\n- In sequence II, the limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{3}{n}=0.\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\n- In sequence III, the dominant term in the denominator is ${n}^{3},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2{n}^{2}-3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}+5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{n}^{2}}{{n}^{3}}-\\frac{3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{n}-3)}{(1+\\frac{5}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-3}{1+0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\nIn conclusion, sequence I diverges." 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406406 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406407 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406406 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406408 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406406 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406409 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406406 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406410 13194139533507 17592186045585 true] [17592186406406 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406411 13194139533507 17592186045585 